      "obligation": "refuter:16:1",
      "id": "cex-equal-central-character-does-not-give-every-verma-embedding-direction",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 assumes only a “regular dominant $A_2$ dot orbit,” but the dependency definition requires every linkage edge to satisfy $\\langle\\eta_{j-1}+\\rho,\\alpha_j^\\vee\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$. Thus BGG does not give the asserted embedding without positive integrality. For example take shifted simple-coroot pairings $\\sqrt2,\\sqrt3$; this is regular dominant, but none of $\\sqrt2,\\sqrt3,\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3$ is a positive integer, so no nonempty strong-linkage edge starts at $\\lambda$ and $M(s_1\\cdot\\lambda)\\hookrightarrow M(\\lambda)$ does not follow.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "0c69546f495a78fadd6e70d93533ff3f7fc06ce4767e07f2cd5094ab39f80a10"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:16:2",
      "id": "ex-a2-regular-dominant-verma-embedding-poset",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement claims the Bruhat embedding poset for every regular dominant $\\lambda$, while strong linkage requires positive-integral coroot pairings. Take type $A_2$ with $\\langle\\lambda+\\rho,\\alpha_1^\\vee\\rangle=\\sqrt2$ and $\\langle\\lambda+\\rho,\\alpha_2^\\vee\\rangle=\\sqrt3$. Then $\\lambda$ is regular dominant and $e\\le s_1$ in Bruhat order, but no positive root has a positive-integral pairing, so the BGG criterion gives no embedding $M(s_1\\cdot\\lambda)\\hookrightarrow M(\\lambda)$. The omitted integral-dominance hypothesis makes the stated poset false.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "f351665d98ab1be5a950b1528fb2814c7d4dfaaaade67bc0f14578f71d4550dd"
    }
  ],
